     "part_tier": null,
     "problem_md": "The Earth orbits the Sun at an average distance of $1 . 51 \\times 10^{8} \\text{km}$ and takes $365 . 25 \\text{days}$ for one orbit.\n\nCalculate the orbital speed of the Earth around the Sun in $\\text{km/h}$.",
     "solution_md": "To calculate the orbital speed of the Earth:\n\n- List the known quantities:\n\n- Radius of orbit, r = 1.51 × 10<sup>8</sup> km\n- Time for one orbit, T = 365.25 days\n\n- Convert the time to hours:\n\n$T = 365 . 25 \\times 24$ **[1 mark]**\n\n$T = 8766 \\text{h}$\n\n- Write down the equation:\n\n$v = \\frac{2πr}{T}$\n\n- Substitute the known values:\n\n$v = \\frac{2π\\times1.51\\times10^{8}}{8766}$ **[1 mark]**\n\n- Calculate the answer:\n\nv = **1.08 × 10**<sup>**5**</sup>** km/h** **[1 mark]**\n\n> **[mark-scheme]**\n> 1 mark for correctly converting 365.25 days to 8766 hours\n> \n> 1 mark for correct substitution into $v = \\frac{2πr}{T}$ (accept formula in any form)\n> \n> 1 mark for a correct final answer of 1.08 × 10<sup>5</sup> km/h\n\n> **[exam-tip]**\n> Always check the units required by the question — here the answer needs to be in km/h, so make sure you convert the orbital period from days into hours before substituting into the equation.",
